1
00:00:02,734 --> 00:00:11,720
Muscle cells, or fibres, are packed with two protein filaments, actin and myosin, that slide to make the cell shorten.

2
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:13,608
There are three kinds.

3
00:00:13,608 --> 00:00:22,006
Striated, or skeletal, muscle: long unbranched cylinders, striped, with many nuclei at the edge.

4
00:00:22,006 --> 00:00:25,801
It is voluntary, fast, powerful, and it tires.

5
00:00:26,034 --> 00:00:38,197
Cardiac muscle, only in the heart: striped too, but its fibres branch, each has one central nucleus, and neighbouring cells are joined end to end by intercalated discs.

6
00:00:38,197 --> 00:00:41,638
It is involuntary, rhythmic, and it never tires.

7
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:48,275
And smooth muscle: spindle shaped cells, one central nucleus, no stripes.

8
00:00:48,275 --> 00:00:56,334
It is involuntary, slow and steady, in the walls of the gut, the uterus, the blood vessels and the iris.

9
00:00:57,567 --> 00:01:06,034
The three way table for the exam: fibre shape, striations, nuclei, intercalated discs, control, and fatigue.

10
00:01:08,767 --> 00:01:13,508
Under high power, striated muscle is a stack of repeating units.

11
00:01:13,508 --> 00:01:22,115
Between two Z lines lies one sarcomere, the unit of contraction, about two point four micrometres long at rest.

12
00:01:22,115 --> 00:01:27,232
Thick myosin filaments sit in the middle and make the dark A band.

13
00:01:27,232 --> 00:01:32,506
Thin actin filaments are anchored to the Z lines and make the light I band.

14
00:01:32,506 --> 00:01:36,396
The middle of the A band, with myosin only, is the H zone.

15
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:37,852
Now contract.

16
00:01:37,852 --> 00:01:43,255
The actin filaments slide in over the myosin, pulling the Z lines together.

17
00:01:43,255 --> 00:01:44,570
Watch the bands.

18
00:01:44,570 --> 00:01:46,172
The I band shrinks.

19
00:01:46,172 --> 00:01:48,867
The H zone shrinks and can vanish.

20
00:01:48,867 --> 00:01:55,000
But the A band does not change at all, because the myosin filaments themselves never shorten.

21
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:55,746
They just slide.

22
00:01:56,967 --> 00:01:57,848
A worked example.

23
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:00,207
Put everything in micrometres.

24
00:02:00,207 --> 00:02:07,030
Two point four centimetres is twenty four thousand micrometres, so there are ten thousand sarcomeres in a row.

25
00:02:07,234 --> 00:02:14,637
Each shrinks to one point eight micrometres: eighteen thousand micrometres, one point eight centimetres.

26
00:02:14,867 --> 00:02:17,199
A shortening of twenty five percent.

27
00:02:17,199 --> 00:02:25,362
Each sarcomere moves only zero point six micrometres, but ten thousand in series add up to a movement you can see.

28
00:02:27,767 --> 00:02:28,927
Pause and think.

29
00:02:28,927 --> 00:02:30,536
Which band stays the same length?

30
00:02:37,434 --> 00:02:44,402
The A band, because it is exactly the length of the myosin filaments, and they never shorten.

31
00:02:44,402 --> 00:02:51,310
The I band and H zone both shrink as actin slides in, and the Z lines move closer together.

32
00:02:55,667 --> 00:03:04,861
Nervous tissue is built from neurons, which carry messages, and the more numerous neuroglia, which support, insulate and protect them.

33
00:03:04,861 --> 00:03:14,348
A typical motor neuron has a cell body, the cyton, with its nucleus and dark patches of rough endoplasmic reticulum, the Nissl granules.

34
00:03:14,567 --> 00:03:19,217
Short branched dendrites carry impulses towards the cyton.

35
00:03:19,217 --> 00:03:26,737
One long axon carries them away, ending in fine branches, the telodendria, tipped with synaptic knobs.

36
00:03:26,967 --> 00:03:32,984
In many neurons the axon is wrapped in a fatty myelin sheath, made by Schwann cells.

37
00:03:32,984 --> 00:03:38,162
The sheath is broken every millimetre or so, at the nodes of Ranvier.

38
00:03:38,367 --> 00:03:40,048
And here is why it matters.

39
00:03:40,048 --> 00:03:43,746
The impulse cannot travel under the insulating myelin.

40
00:03:43,746 --> 00:03:47,444
It jumps from node to node: saltatory conduction.

41
00:03:47,444 --> 00:03:51,046
That makes a myelinated fibre up to a hundred times faster.

42
00:03:52,667 --> 00:03:54,843
Race them over the same distance.

43
00:03:54,843 --> 00:04:00,325
Myelinated, eighty five metres per second, is across in a flash.

44
00:04:00,325 --> 00:04:04,554
Non myelinated, half a metre per second, is still crawling.

45
00:04:05,767 --> 00:04:07,255
Now the cold open, solved.

46
00:04:07,467 --> 00:04:10,489
The nerve path is one point seven metres.

47
00:04:10,489 --> 00:04:16,580
At eighty five metres per second that takes zero point zero two seconds: twenty milliseconds.

48
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:21,888
Add two synaptic delays of half a millisecond each: twenty one milliseconds.

49
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:27,701
Along a bare fibre at half a metre per second: three point four seconds.

50
00:04:27,701 --> 00:04:30,318
About a hundred and sixty times slower.

51
00:04:30,318 --> 00:04:33,483
The myelin is the difference between a scratch and a deep wound.

52
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:38,969
Where one neuron meets the next is a synapse.

53
00:04:38,969 --> 00:04:46,248
The membranes never touch: a gap of about twenty nanometres, the synaptic cleft, separates them.

54
00:04:46,248 --> 00:04:53,687
When an impulse reaches the knob, vesicles release a chemical, the neurotransmitter, such as acetylcholine.

55
00:04:53,900 --> 00:05:00,256
It diffuses across the cleft, binds receptors on the next cell, and starts a new impulse there.

56
00:05:00,256 --> 00:05:03,771
An enzyme, cholinesterase, then destroys it.

57
00:05:03,771 --> 00:05:11,416
Because the vesicles are only on one side and the receptors only on the other, a synapse works in one direction only.

58
00:05:13,034 --> 00:05:20,900
Neurons are named by their processes: unipolar, bipolar as in the retina, and multipolar, the commonest.

59
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:24,689
By job: sensory, motor and interneurons.

60
00:05:24,689 --> 00:05:41,069
And the neuroglia: astrocytes, which help form the blood brain barrier; oligodendrocytes, which make myelin inside the brain and spinal cord; microglia, the nervous system's phagocytes; and ependymal cells, which line the ventricles.

61
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:44,269
Exam tip.

62
00:05:44,269 --> 00:05:46,209
Do not mix up the two sheaths.

63
00:05:46,209 --> 00:05:50,975
The myelin sheath is the fatty insulation made by the Schwann cell.

64
00:05:50,975 --> 00:05:57,003
The neurilemma is the Schwann cell's own thin outer membrane, outside the myelin.

65
00:05:57,003 --> 00:06:03,018
Non myelinated fibres still have a neurilemma; they just have no myelin and no nodes.

66
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:06,160
Pause and think.

67
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:08,151
Where will you never find Nissl granules?

68
00:06:15,034 --> 00:06:16,116
In the axon.

69
00:06:16,116 --> 00:06:25,226
Nissl granules are found in the cyton and the dendrites, and their absence from the axon is exactly how you tell an axon from a dendrite under the microscope.

70
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:28,521
Quick recap.

71
00:06:28,521 --> 00:06:33,717
Epithelium is packed cells on a basement membrane, with no blood vessels.

72
00:06:33,717 --> 00:06:37,610
Connective tissue is few cells in a lot of matrix.

73
00:06:37,610 --> 00:06:43,912
Tendons are collagen and join muscle to bone; ligaments are elastic and join bone to bone.

74
00:06:43,912 --> 00:06:48,808
Cartilage has no blood supply; mammalian bone has Haversian systems.

75
00:06:48,808 --> 00:06:55,891
Muscle is striated, cardiac or smooth, and in a contracting sarcomere the A band stays the same.

76
00:06:55,891 --> 00:07:04,790
And in a neuron, dendrites bring impulses in, the axon carries them out, myelin makes them jump, and the synapse is one way.

77
00:07:08,967 --> 00:07:10,700
That was animal tissues.

78
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:18,083
Next, developmental biology: how sperm and eggs are made, and how a frog's egg becomes a tadpole.

79
00:07:18,083 --> 00:07:18,698
See you there.

